Build a multi-agent "Architecture Review Board" for Claude Code in this repo.

1. Five subagents in .claude/agents/ (model: sonnet; tools: Read, Grep, Glob, WebSearch, WebFetch):
   solution-architect (Sol: domain boundaries, one owner per entity),
   infra-architect (Ian: 10x load, no single point of failure),
   security-architect (Sam: no PII exposure, can veto),
   cost-architect (Cora: $2k/month budget, line-item cost math, can veto),
   pragmatist-architect (Pat: 4 devs, ship in 6 weeks, pushback gentle/normal/brutal).
   Each has a non-negotiable and a known blind spot. Rules: be concrete, raise at least 2
   objections per design with a failure scenario, may concede, end every answer with the
   JSON block the moderator asks for.

2. A skill at .claude/skills/arch-review/SKILL.md (/arch-review <problem> [--preset
   startup|enterprise|bank|game] [--quick] [--pushback ...]). The main session is the chair
   and runs: clarify questions -> 5 blind proposals in parallel -> human checkpoint ->
   anonymous critique (designs shuffled and labeled A-E; one event per critic-design pair;
   rerun once if the critique is too soft) -> revise -> vote on 5 weighted criteria
   (scale, security, cost, simplicity, speed) with vetoes and a pre-mortem -> decision
   (winner or hybrid, consensus strong/contested/suspicious, dissent list, risk register).
   Write ADR.md, and don't mark the meeting done until ADR.md exists.
   After every step, rewrite design-reviews/<date>-<slug>/meeting-data.js as
   window.MEETING = {...} (events with seq/phase/type/from/to/design/severity/title/body,
   plus designs, votes, criteria, decision, dissent, risks, pendingQuestion).

3. viewer.html (copied into each meeting folder as index.html), which re-reads
   meeting-data.js every 3 seconds and shows:
   - an animated SVG circle of the agents with the chair in the center and the human on top;
     arrows colored by message type, with a replay slider and play controls
   - a transcript grouped by phase, with agent filters
   - design cards with Mermaid diagrams
   - a weighted decision matrix and a heatmap of who scored what
   - the final decision with dissent and risks
   - a short description of each agent
   It supports light and dark mode.

4. server.py (Python standard library only, 127.0.0.1:8765) with a start page where I type a
   problem, choose the preset, pushback and quick mode, and see past meetings.
   POST /api/start creates the meeting folder and runs, in the background:
     claude -p "/arch-review ... --meeting-dir <dir> --web" --permission-mode acceptEdits
       --output-format stream-json --verbose --disallowedTools AskUserQuestion
       --allowedTools Agent Read Write Edit Glob Grep WebSearch WebFetch
       "Bash(sh .claude/skills/arch-review/wait-answer.sh:*)"
   with the output going to run.log. In --web mode the chair asks questions by writing
   pendingQuestion into meeting-data.js; the viewer shows an answer form that posts to
   /api/answer, which writes answers/<id>.json; wait-answer.sh blocks until that file exists
   (Bash timeout 600000). Also add /api/status (a banner with the last log message if the
   chair dies) and /api/resume, which relaunches with --resume so the chair continues from
   the last completed round.

5. Include a sample meeting in design-reviews/sample/ so the viewer can be tested without
   running agents. Then test the viewer in a browser and run one --quick meeting end to end.
